 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186339007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Inverse of a Larger Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087700 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a75c67e5-3469-5730-9181-54df4ec6d5b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1045 "Introduction to the Inverse of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087930 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e07e7ccb-9045-51ac-ad31-1220ff860794" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088524 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088524 #edb/ref 1061 16452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088524 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "390345b1-ff60-5803-bf48-ae86115b2de2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088524 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088525 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088525 #edb/ref 1061 16453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088525 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "42dd802f-22c6-5586-8fae-028b66defc18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088525 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088526 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088526 #edb/ref 1061 16454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088526 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3941120f-c21f-51b0-b616-5b3059c55fc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088526 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088527 #edb/ref 1061 16456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088527 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46575a16-9bf0-5f72-8c33-a6d4bf578d86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088527 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088893 #edb/ref 1064 "Given the scalar $a,$ the **multiplicative inverse** of $a$ is defined as the scalar $a^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot a^{-1} = a^{-1} \\cdot a = 1.\n$$\n\nFor example, the multiplicative inverse of $2$ is the number $2^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ because \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 =  2\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, given a square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **inverse** of $A$ is defined as the $n \\times n$ matrix $A^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^{-1} = A^{-1} \\cdot A = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the following $2\\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe inverse of the above matrix is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo verify that the above matrix is in fact the inverse of $A,$ first observe that if we multiply $A$ by the above matrix $A^{-1},$ then we get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1 & -1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply $A^{-1}$ by $A,$ then we also get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A} & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe do not yet know how to find the inverse of a matrix. However, we can verify that one matrix is the inverse of the other by carrying out the same procedure as we did here." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088893 #edb/ref 1065 3043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088893 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "427bce08-8aa6-508f-969b-2a2ed4eb8c7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088893 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088894 #edb/ref 1064 "The number $0$ does not have an inverse for multiplication over the real numbers, because there's no such number $a$ that $a \\cdot 0 = 1.$ Instead, for every $a$ we have $a \\cdot 0 = 0.$\n\nSimilarly, not every matrix has an inverse.\n\n- If a matrix is not square, an inverse does not exist.\n\n- Some square matrices do not have an inverse. If a square matrix does not have an inverse, then it is said to be **singular**. An example of a singular matrix is the zero matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe will soon learn how to calculate the inverse of a square matrix, and we'll also soon learn how to determine when a square matrix does not have an inverse." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088894 #edb/ref 1065 3044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088894 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fea084a4-999a-5a17-8f35-e8d7e5dbf25c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088894 #edb/ref 1067 "Not Every Matrix Has an Inverse" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1